          %p
            Discussion of use of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complexes"} simplicial complexes
            in graph theory:
          %ul
            %li
              MO,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/161586/what-have-simplicial-complexes-ever-done-for-graph-theory"} What have simplicial complexes ever done for graph theory?
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  The last condition is to ensure that
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi W
                        %mo ,
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (W,F)
                  is itself again a graph, which would not be guaranteed if we defined subgraph to be just any pair of subsets of the respective sets
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi E
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:2
                %p
                  This happens to be the usual notion of substructure in model theory, for any relational structure.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:2", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:3
                %p
                  Somewhat counterintuitively (with regard to connotations of the words
                  %em spanning
                  and
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %em induced
                  %em spanning
                  subgraph need not be
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em induced
                  %em except
                  if the subgraph is the graph itself.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:3", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:4
                %p
                  We here follow A. Bondy’s choice of words in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#HandbookOfCombinatoricsVol1"} p. 20
                  %em hamiltonian
                  or
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em Hamilton
                  %em cycle
                  or
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em circuit
                  %em circuit
                  is less usual than
                  %em cycle
                  in combinatorics, but less ambiguous, not longer, and more clearly signalling that the combinatorial notion is meant (not one of the many other meanings of
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %em cycle
                  %em Hamilton
                  is that
                  %em any
                  circuit, by itself, is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %em hamiltonian
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:4", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
              %li#fn:5
                %p
                  Incidentally, the term
                  %em full
                  was in use in mid-twentieth century graph theory, then seems to have fallen out of favor.
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:5", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
